taking a comics character from the  printed page to the big screen isn't as  easy as you might think in guardians of  the galaxy's Gamora was no exception  just ask Charlie Wynne former head of  visual development at Marvel Studios and  take a look at some of the designs that  didn't make the cut before 2014 there's  a good chance that if you set the phrase  guardians of the galaxy to your average  moviegoer their reaction would be pretty  similar across the board and that's  exactly what Charlie Wynn was banking on  when he started adapting the characters  from Marvel Comics long history of  spacefaring weirdos for guardians of the  galaxy nobody knew what guardians was  it's very niche and so what was great  was it also left it so that we could do  a lot with it if you upset some of the  hardcore fans maybe it's okay because  there's not as many of them you know  what it did do was allowed for a lot of  exploration to create a Marvel Space  Odyssey the freedom to experiment is  probably why the screen version of  Gamora as well as the concept art Wynne  created has very little in common with  the comic-book depictions that have  appeared in the pages of Marvel Comics  over the years some comic book  characters are relatively easy to adapt  to the screen at least when it comes to  knowing what to keep and what to junk  Batman spider-man and Wonder Woman may  have outlandish comic-book outfits but  they also feature iconic details that  make them recognizable Captain America  without his shield and super outfit  suddenly becomes a guy who's a little  too excited every July 4th and Wolverine  without his claws is just your hairy  uncle Lou back with another case of  molson  Gamora by contrast doesn't really have  any features that truly define her look  in the minds of comic book fans and that  presented a major design challenge for  the team where do you begin for when it  started with archetypes but it didn't  end there as an archetype she's in this  the lone assassin that functions on her  own and you start there but then I think  you really need to add in those elements  that she needs to service while this  concept art doesn't seem too out there  it also doesn't really communicate many  ideas about who Gamora is or what she  wants her needs  the Moroz adventuring gear seems like  she's ready for battle but we can't tell  much about her character  on that according to win an essential  part of the design process comes not  simply from picking the right look for  the character in fact it's quite the  opposite  we're trying to write some kind of story  paint an image one of the things that  you do first is you figure out what your  limits are the farthest you're gonna go  where where are you gonna start with and  so it's your contrast what is your  contrast that idea of establishing the  contrast is clearly at play in this  concept art where we see Gomorrah  looking a whole lot different from any  of her depictions either on screen or on  the page when led the team about as far  away as they could from the comics just  to see how far they could stretch the  idea of Gomorrah before it broke down  with her purple skin weirdly tapered  ears and her dimpled mostly bald skull  these versions of Gomorrah definitely  have a passing resemblance to her  adopted dad Thanos but she feels a lot  more unsettling and creepy than you  might expect for a big-budget Marvel  movie hero as when directed his team to  continue exploring different looks for  gamora's on-screen appearance they  started moving in interesting new  directions case in point this light  purple version of Gamora whose skin  features some of the intricate markings  that would become a part of the final  design signature look more importantly  however is the expression on her face  which seems to express a lot more of  what might be going on inside her head  when told us that Kevin Feige was very  interested in this particular look for  Gamora but ultimately they decided it  was more difficult to pull off the  intricate tech on her face than it was  worth as gamora's look evolved her  character began to emerge in the concept  art Charlie Wynne created in these  images we see two similar settings and  scenarios for the character Gamora in a  crowded sci-fi locale surrounded by  aliens and other outerspace weirdness  but you can see that even though the  Gamora in this image is undoubtedly cool  and on the hunt this other one that  looks lost in thought seems to have a  lot more in common with the version of  the character we got in guardians of the  galaxy  you know she's this super assassin  that's from a different alien race  connected with vanos we're onin nebula  so she's got again she's got her own  dysfunctional family to worry about she  has to have  vulnerabilities even just in her look  they can't just go for this ninja right  that's all covered and everything  there's a part of her that is exposed  and I think we're vulnerable paints it a  little bit better also that she needs to  be a character that you can relate to  because she's gonna have that kind of  connection with Peter quill you know  later on while this image still doesn't  look at ton like the finalized look for  Gamora her purple skin is a dead  giveaway it still manages to express  more about the character in the world of  guardians of the galaxy than any of the  previous versions seen Gamora at the bar  with her Cheshire Cat grin seems to make  her look dangerous and deadly but also  ready to get drunk thanks to the forearm  barkeep right behind her what's most  important is that this version of Gamora  has tons of personality that's the most  important part when designing meaningful  characters that audiences will spend a  lot of time with how do you make her  feel like the character that you would  really feel for but there's something  happens to considering how fans reacted  when she died at the hands of fan else  in Avengers infinity war it goes without  